/* Neurosciences, Neuropathology and applied neuroscience. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).neuro = (window.PA_CARDS.neuro || []).concat([
/* ---------- Dementia and neurodegeneration ---------- */
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","What protein makes up the neurofibrillary tangles of Alzheimer's disease?","Abnormally hyperphosphorylated tau","Tangles are cytoskeletal: tau that is too heavily phosphorylated falls off microtubules and self-assembles."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","What is the normal job of tau protein in a neuron?","It binds and stabilises microtubules","Microtubules carry material along the axon, so losing tau also wrecks axonal transport."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","Which pathological change correlates best with the severity of cognitive decline in Alzheimer's disease?","The burden of neurofibrillary tangles","Plaque counts correlate poorly; tangle spread tracks neuronal loss, which is what causes symptoms."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","What does Braak and Braak staging of Alzheimer's disease describe?","The spread of tangles from entorhinal cortex to isocortex","Staging follows a fixed anatomical sequence, so it can be used to grade severity at autopsy."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","Which single structure contains the greatest density of tangles in established Alzheimer's disease?","The hippocampus","Tangles also appear in amygdala, parahippocampal gyrus, neocortex, locus coeruleus and raphe nuclei."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: tangles","Are neurofibrillary tangles specific to Alzheimer's disease?","No, they occur in normal ageing and other disorders","Tangles are also seen in dementia pugilistica and progressive supranuclear palsy, so density and site matter."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: plaques","What is the main protein of the core of a neuritic plaque?","Beta-amyloid (A-beta 42)","Amyloid is extracellular, unlike tangles, which sit inside the neuron."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: plaques","Which secretase cleaves amyloid precursor protein in a way that prevents amyloid formation?","Alpha-secretase","Alpha cleavage cuts within the amyloid sequence, so no intact A-beta peptide is released."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: plaques","Which two enzymes acting together generate beta-amyloid from its precursor?","Beta-secretase and gamma-secretase","Sequential cutting at both ends liberates the sticky A-beta fragment that aggregates."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: plaques","Where are senile plaques found compared with tangles?","In broadly the same regions, such as hippocampus and neocortex","Both follow the limbic and association cortex, which are the areas that are hit earliest."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: macroscopic","Name a macroscopic change of Alzheimer's disease seen on gross inspection of the brain.","Enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles","Plaques and tangles are microscopic; atrophy, low brain weight and wide sulci are the naked-eye findings."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: macroscopic","Which lobe shows the earliest atrophy on structural MRI in Alzheimer's disease?","The medial temporal lobe, especially hippocampus","Atrophy mirrors tangle spread, which begins in medial temporal structures."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: other inclusions","Hirano bodies are made of which protein?","Actin and actin-associated proteins","They are rod-shaped eosinophilic cytoplasmic bodies, most often in hippocampal pyramidal cells."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: other inclusions","A brain autopsy of a patient with dementia shows Hirano bodies in the hippocampus. Which dementia is most likely?","Alzheimer's disease","Hirano bodies occur in ageing but are numerous in Alzheimer's disease."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: risk","Which apolipoprotein E allele is protective against Alzheimer's disease?","The e2 allele","Apolipoprotein E handles lipid transport and amyloid clearance; e2 clears better than e4."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: biomarkers","What is the typical CSF profile in Alzheimer's disease?","Low beta-amyloid 42 with raised total and phosphorylated tau","Amyloid is trapped in plaques so CSF levels fall, while dying neurons release tau."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alzheimer's: biomarkers","A 70-year-old woman in a memory clinic has mild cognitive impairment. Which CSF measure best predicts progression to dementia?","A raised tau to amyloid-beta ratio","Combining the two markers captures both plaque load and neuronal injury, so it predicts better than either alone."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","Alpha-synuclein is the major constituent of which inclusion?","The Lewy body","Lewy bodies also hold ubiquitin, neurofilament protein and granular material."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","Describe the appearance of a Lewy body under the microscope.","A spherical eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion with a pale halo","The halo is the ring of displaced cytoplasm around the dense core of aggregated protein."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","Autopsy in an elderly man with tremor and progressive cognitive decline shows tau-negative neuronal inclusions. What is the likely diagnosis?","Dementia with Lewy bodies","Lewy bodies are made of alpha-synuclein and ubiquitin and so do not stain for tau."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","In dementia with Lewy bodies, where is the density of Lewy bodies highest?","Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus and temporal cortex","Cortical spread distinguishes it from Parkinson's disease, where brainstem involvement dominates early."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","What is the key macroscopic change in idiopathic Parkinson's disease?","Depigmentation of the substantia nigra, especially zona compacta","Loss of the neuromelanin-containing dopaminergic cells makes the nigra look pale."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Lewy body disorders","Which other pigmented brainstem nucleus is depigmented in Parkinson's disease?","The locus coeruleus","Noradrenergic loss there contributes to depression and poor arousal in Parkinson's disease."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","Knife-blade atrophy of the gyri is characteristic of which dementia?","Pick's disease","Severe frontal and anterior temporal atrophy thins the gyri while parietal and occipital lobes are spared."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","What are Pick bodies?","Round argyrophilic intraneuronal tau inclusions","They are the defining microscopic finding, alongside neuronal loss and reactive astrocytosis."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","Which immunostain is most useful to detect Pick bodies?","Anti-tau antibody (with anti-ubiquitin)","Pick bodies are tau-positive, so tau immunohistochemistry is both sensitive and specific."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","What are Pick cells?","Swollen, pale cortical pyramidal neurons (balloon cells)","The cell body swells as the cytoskeleton fails, giving a ballooned appearance."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","In which condition are both balloon cells and Hirano bodies found together?","Pick's disease","Hirano bodies are not specific to Alzheimer's disease and accompany balloon cells in Pick's disease."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","A 59-year-old man seen in a memory clinic has progressive word-finding difficulty and loss of word meaning, with good day-to-day memory. What is the likely diagnosis?","Semantic dementia, a temporal variant of frontotemporal dementia","Anterior temporal lobe stores word meaning, so semantic knowledge goes while episodic memory is spared."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Frontotemporal dementia","In semantic dementia, which type of verbal fluency is relatively preserved?","Letter (phonological) fluency, while category fluency is poor","Category fluency depends on semantic stores in the anterior temporal lobe, which are degraded."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Other tauopathies","Which clinical sign strongly suggests progressive supranuclear palsy?","Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy with early falls","Tau pathology hits the midbrain gaze centres and brainstem, so eye movements are not spared."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Other tauopathies","What is the microscopic hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy?","Neurofibrillary tangles of tau in basal ganglia and brainstem","It is a tauopathy, which is why it is grouped with frontotemporal degenerations."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Vascular dementia","In which dementia are arteriosclerotic changes in major cerebral arteries most commonly found?","Multi-infarct dementia","Repeated infarction from diseased vessels causes patchy damage, atrophy and ventricular enlargement."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Vascular dementia","A 73-year-old man attends the memory clinic with stepwise decline, patchy deficits, focal signs and relatively preserved insight and personality. What is the likely diagnosis?","Vascular dementia","Damage is scattered along vascular territories, so some abilities are lost while others stay intact."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Vascular dementia","Binswanger's disease affects which part of the brain?","Deep subcortical white matter","Small vessel disease causes diffuse white matter ischaemia with a slow, apathetic subcortical picture."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Huntington's disease","Which structure shows marked atrophy in Huntington's disease?","The caudate nucleus (and corpus striatum)","Loss of striatal medium spiny neurons flattens the caudate head, giving box-car ventricles."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Huntington's disease","A 45-year-old man presents to the CMHT with depression and early cognitive change; his father developed dementia at 60. What EEG change would support Huntington's disease?","Generalised reduction of alpha activity, later a flat trace","Widespread cortical and striatal neuronal loss lowers the amplitude of all rhythms."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","A 55-year-old woman who drinks heavily cannot form new memories but holds a conversation. Where is the most prominent pathology?","The dorsomedial nucleus of the thalamus","Thiamine deficiency damages periventricular diencephalon, which is the relay for episodic memory."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","Which structures show petechial haemorrhage and gliosis in Wernicke's encephalopathy?","Mammillary bodies and periventricular grey matter around the third ventricle and aqueduct","These regions have high thiamine turnover and so fail first."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","Which type of memory is chiefly impaired in Korsakoff's syndrome?","Episodic memory","Procedural and semantic memory depend on other circuits and are relatively preserved."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","Roughly what proportion of alcohol-dependent patients recovering from Wernicke's encephalopathy develop Korsakoff's syndrome?","About 80 per cent","Acute thiamine deficiency often leaves fixed diencephalic damage, so amnesia persists after confusion settles."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","Which structure degenerates in Marchiafava-Bignami disease?","The corpus callosum","Demyelination of the callosum in chronic alcohol use causes dementia, dysarthria and disconnection signs."],
["Dementia and neurodegeneration","Alcohol-related pathology","What is confabulation thought to reflect?","Faulty temporal ordering and monitoring of real memories","Fragments are retrieved out of context and not checked, so they are reported as recent events."],
/* ---------- Prion disease ---------- */
["Prion disease","Pathology","A 33-year-old man develops rapidly progressive cognitive decline with myoclonus, ataxia and then akinetic mutism. What change is expected at brain autopsy?","Extensive vacuole formation (spongiform change)","Vacuolation of the neuropil gives the sponge-like appearance that names the disease."],
["Prion disease","Pathology","What causes the spongiform appearance of brain tissue in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","Vacuolation of the neuropil","The holes are in the dendritic and axonal felt, not in dead cells, so no inflammation is seen."],
["Prion disease","Pathology","Name three microscopic features of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease.","Spongiform change, neuronal loss and astrocytic proliferation","Striking is the absence of inflammation, because prions provoke no immune response."],
["Prion disease","Pathology","Which dementia may show selective cerebellar atrophy at autopsy?","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","Prion deposition is often heavy in cerebellum, which explains early ataxia."],
["Prion disease","EEG and imaging","What is the classic EEG finding in sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","Periodic generalised sharp waves at 1 to 2 Hz on a slow background","Synchronised cortical discharges appear as the cortex is progressively destroyed."],
["Prion disease","EEG and imaging","Is the classic periodic EEG seen in variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","No","Variant disease has a different distribution of pathology, so the EEG is usually non-specific."],
["Prion disease","EEG and imaging","Which MRI sign suggests variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","The pulvinar sign, high signal in the posterior thalamus","Prion deposition is heaviest in posterior thalamus in the variant form."],
["Prion disease","Variant CJD","How does variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease differ from sporadic disease in age?","It affects much younger people, with average age at death around 30","Dietary exposure to bovine prions occurred in young people, and incubation is long but not lifelong."],
["Prion disease","Variant CJD","Which biopsy can support a diagnosis of variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","Tonsillar biopsy","Lymphoreticular tissue accumulates prion protein in the variant form, unlike sporadic disease."],
["Prion disease","Variant CJD","Which plaque type is typical of variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease?","Florid plaques","These are amyloid plaques surrounded by vacuoles, a daisy-like pattern unique to the variant form."],
/* ---------- Epilepsy and EEG ---------- */
["Epilepsy and EEG","Origin of the EEG","What generates most of the electrical activity recorded on scalp EEG?","Postsynaptic currents flowing along the dendrites of cortical pyramidal neurons","Pyramidal cells are aligned in parallel, so their slow synaptic currents summate; action potentials are too brief."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Origin of the EEG","Why does magnetoencephalography detect mainly tangential currents?","Radial currents produce magnetic fields that stay inside the head","By the right-hand rule, only currents parallel to the scalp produce fields that escape it."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Origin of the EEG","Which imaging method needs a liquid helium tank to keep its sensors superconducting?","Magnetoencephalography","SQUID detectors only work near absolute zero, so they are bathed in liquid helium."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Normal rhythms","What is the frequency and site of the normal alpha rhythm?","8 to 13 Hz, posteriorly, with eyes closed","Alpha is the idling rhythm of visual cortex and is blocked when the eyes open."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Normal rhythms","What EEG frequency band is beta, and where is it most prominent?","Above 13 Hz, frontally","Beta reflects desynchronised cortical activity during alertness."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Normal rhythms","Which drug group typically increases beta activity on the EEG?","Benzodiazepines","Enhanced GABA-A transmission drives fast, low-amplitude cortical activity."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Normal rhythms","What are the frequency ranges of theta and delta activity?","Theta 4 to 7 Hz and delta below 4 Hz","Slower rhythms appear with drowsiness, deep sleep and cortical dysfunction."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","A 68-year-old man on an acute ward develops diffuse slowing of background rhythm with confusion. What does this indicate?","Delirium","Any diffuse metabolic insult slows cortical rhythms, which is why slowing is the general marker of delirium."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","A 68-year-old man with alcohol dependence develops tremor, sweating and visual hallucinations on a medical ward. What EEG is expected?","Fast activity, or a normal EEG","Delirium tremens is a state of withdrawal hyperexcitability, so the EEG is not slowed like other deliria."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","Which EEG pattern is characteristic of hepatic encephalopathy?","Triphasic slow waves at about 1.5 to 3 Hz","Ammonia and other toxins produce a stereotyped high-voltage slow discharge."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","Which EEG pattern suggests herpes simplex encephalitis?","Periodic discharges every 1 to 3 seconds with focal waves over the temporal lobes","The virus has a temporal lobe tropism, so the discharges are lateralised there."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","What EEG pattern is seen in myoclonic epilepsy?","Generalised polyspike and wave discharges","Multiple spikes drive the brief muscle jerks of myoclonus."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","What EEG pattern defines childhood absence epilepsy?","Generalised 3 Hz spike and wave activity","Thalamocortical circuits oscillate in a rhythmic, highly synchronised way."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","What does focal slow wave activity on an EEG suggest?","An underlying structural lesion","Local tissue damage slows the rhythms of the cortex above it, so imaging is needed."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","A 32-year-old woman is detained under Section 136 after episodic aggression with confusion and amnesia afterwards. What EEG abnormality may be found?","Lateralised spike and wave activity","Complex partial seizures arise from one temporal lobe, and the post-ictal state explains the amnesia."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Abnormal EEG patterns","Which EEG pattern suggests anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis?","Extreme delta brush","Rhythmic delta with superimposed fast activity is a fairly specific electrographic signature."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","What is post-ictal psychosis?","Psychosis appearing after a lucid interval of hours to days following seizures","The lucid interval separates it from ictal confusion and from chronic interictal psychosis."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","Which epilepsy focus is most associated with chronic interictal schizophrenia-like psychosis?","A left temporal lobe focus","Repeated discharge in dominant limbic cortex is thought to drive abnormal salience and beliefs."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","Name three typical auras of temporal lobe epilepsy.","Epigastric rising sensation, deja vu and olfactory hallucination","The seizure starts in limbic and uncal cortex, which handles memory, smell and visceral sensation."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","A 24-year-old woman in A&E has prolonged shaking with closed eyes, pelvic thrusting, no incontinence and rapid recovery. What is the likely diagnosis?","A dissociative (non-epileptic) seizure","Preserved awareness, resistance to eye opening and absence of a post-ictal state point away from epilepsy."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","Which blood test taken shortly after an event helps distinguish a generalised tonic-clonic seizure from a dissociative seizure?","Serum prolactin","A true generalised seizure causes a transient prolactin rise; a dissociative attack does not."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy and psychiatry","Which antipsychotic most clearly lowers the seizure threshold in a dose-related way?","Clozapine","Risk rises above about 600 mg daily, so slow titration and valproate cover may be needed."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy treatment","Which drugs are first-line in the UK for newly diagnosed focal epilepsy?","Lamotrigine or levetiracetam","Both are effective with fewer interactions, and carbamazepine is now second line."],
["Epilepsy and EEG","Epilepsy treatment","What happens to the seizure threshold over a course of ECT?","It rises","Repeated seizures recruit endogenous anticonvulsant mechanisms, so the stimulus dose often has to rise."],
/* ---------- Neuroimaging in psychiatry ---------- */
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which technique measures task-related changes in blood oxygenation?","Functional MRI using the BOLD signal","Activity raises local blood flow and changes deoxyhaemoglobin, which alters the T2-star signal."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Does fMRI measure neuronal metabolism directly?","No, it measures tissue perfusion and oxygenation","It is an indirect, haemodynamic proxy, so it is slow compared with neuronal firing."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which technique gives direct information about regional brain metabolism by tagging compounds in a metabolic pathway?","Positron emission tomography","Labelled glucose analogues are taken up in proportion to local metabolic demand."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which technique measures receptor binding using radiolabelled agonists or antagonists and single photon isotopes?","Single photon emission computed tomography","Isotopes such as iodine-123 and technetium-99m emit single photons that are mapped by a gamma camera."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which technique measures the resonance of nuclei such as hydrogen and phosphorus to quantify brain chemicals?","Magnetic resonance spectroscopy","Nuclei with an odd number of protons resonate at characteristic frequencies, giving a chemical spectrum."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which spectroscopy marker indexes neuronal integrity?","N-acetyl aspartate","It is concentrated in neurons, so a fall suggests neuronal loss or dysfunction."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","Which technique measures the static magnetic fields produced by brain activity?","Magnetoencephalography","It has millisecond timing like EEG but better spatial localisation of tangential sources."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Techniques","What does diffusion tensor imaging measure?","The direction and coherence of water diffusion, mapping white matter tracts","Water diffuses along axons, so anisotropy reveals fibre orientation and integrity."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Functional connectivity","What does functional connectivity mean?","A correlation in the time course of activity between two separate brain regions","It is statistical, so it can exist even without a direct white matter connection."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Functional connectivity","What frequency of BOLD fluctuation is captured in resting state fMRI?","Below 0.1 Hz","Spontaneous low-frequency fluctuations are correlated within networks at rest."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Name three core nodes of the default mode network.","Medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate cortex and precuneus","The angular gyrus is also included; these areas are most active at rest."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","What cognitive role is attributed to the default mode network?","Introspection, including mind wandering and autobiographical recall","It is engaged by self-referential, stimulus-independent thought rather than external tasks."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Which network is called the task-negative network?","The default mode network","Its regions deactivate when a demanding external task begins."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Name the two main cortical nodes of the salience network.","Anterior insula and dorsal anterior cingulate cortex","They detect behaviourally relevant events, with thalamus and striatum as subcortical partners."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Which network switches the brain from rest to a cognitively engaged state?","The salience network","By flagging salient input it shifts resources from default mode to task-positive networks."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Name the two main nodes of the central executive (frontoparietal) network.","Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and intraparietal sulcus","This network holds and manipulates information for goal-directed tasks."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Brain networks","Is the precuneus part of the salience network?","No, it is a default mode network hub","It sits in the medial superior parietal lobule and is active at rest."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","A 22-year-old man on an acute ward has persecutory delusions and third person auditory hallucinations. Besides ventricular enlargement, what functional finding is expected?","Poor dorsolateral prefrontal activation on executive tasks","Hypofrontality on task underlies the executive and negative symptom burden of schizophrenia."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","Name two structural imaging findings in schizophrenia.","Ventricular enlargement and loss of grey matter in insula, anterior cingulate and medial temporal lobe","Loss of synapses and neuropil, not neurodegeneration, is thought to shrink grey matter."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","Which spectroscopy finding is reported in the prefrontal cortex in schizophrenia?","Reduced N-acetyl aspartate","Lower NAA suggests reduced neuronal integrity in prefrontal cortex."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","Which asymmetry is reversed in schizophrenia?","The normal left larger than right asymmetry of the planum temporale","Loss of this language-related asymmetry suggests disturbed lateralisation in development."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","In healthy right-handed people, which structure is larger on the left?","The planum temporale","It lies on the posterior superior temporal gyrus and serves language, which is left lateralised."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","A 29-year-old woman seen by the crisis team has low mood and poor sleep. Besides white matter hyperintensities, what imaging finding is expected?","Reduced metabolism in prefrontal and anterior cingulate cortex","Depression is associated with underactivity in the circuits that regulate mood and drive."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","What is true of white matter hyperintensities in mood disorder?","They are linked to vascular risk factors","They appear on T2 images in deep subcortical white matter and are commoner in older patients with vascular disease."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","A 10-year-old boy in a special school has stereotyped movements and aggression. Which MRI finding is associated with autism?","Hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis","Cerebellar development is altered, consistent with a neurodevelopmental origin."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Findings in disorders","What is the typical SPECT perfusion pattern in Alzheimer's disease?","Reduced parietotemporal blood flow","Perfusion falls where synaptic activity is lost, mirroring the FDG-PET pattern."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Development","What does gyrification refer to?","The developmental folding of the cortical surface","Folding packs more cortical surface into the skull and is largely complete before birth."],
["Neuroimaging in psychiatry","Development","Which cortical developmental process is essentially complete by the age of two?","Gyrification","Most folding happens in utero, with only late neonatal changes finishing by about two years."],
/* ---------- CNS infection and neuroimmunology ---------- */
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","HIV","How does HIV cross the blood-brain barrier?","Inside infected monocytes and macrophages","This Trojan horse mechanism smuggles the virus past a barrier it cannot cross as a free particle."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","HIV","A 38-year-old man who injects drugs has weight loss and cognitive decline. What is the most probable diagnosis?","HIV-associated dementia","Injecting is a major route of infection, and subcortical dementia is a late complication."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","HIV","What pattern of cognitive impairment does HIV typically cause?","A subcortical pattern with slowing, apathy and poor attention","The virus damages deep white matter and basal ganglia rather than cortex."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","HIV","Which laboratory marker best indicates risk of HIV-related CNS disease?","A low CD4 count","Immune collapse, rather than plasma viral load alone, allows CNS disease to develop."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","HIV","A 40-year-old man with advanced HIV has headache, fever and multiple ring-enhancing lesions on CT. What is the likely cause?","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Reactivated Toxoplasma forms abscesses with surrounding oedema, which enhance in a ring."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Syphilis","What is general paralysis of the insane?","Tertiary neurosyphilis causing dementia, grandiosity and dysarthria","Treponemal invasion of frontal cortex produces dementia with disinhibition."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Syphilis","A 60-year-old man has small irregular pupils that accommodate but do not react to light. What is this sign?","Argyll Robertson pupils","Damage to the pretectal light reflex pathway spares the near reflex pathway."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Syphilis","What is the treatment of neurosyphilis?","High-dose intravenous or intramuscular benzylpenicillin","Treponema pallidum remains penicillin sensitive, but CNS disease needs high tissue levels."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Syphilis","Absent ankle jerks with upgoing plantars suggest which condition?","Subacute combined degeneration of the cord","Posterior column and corticospinal damage coexist with loss of the peripheral reflex arc."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Viral encephalitis","Which lobes are preferentially damaged in herpes simplex encephalitis?","The temporal lobes, especially medially","Spread along olfactory and trigeminal routes delivers virus to medial temporal structures."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Viral encephalitis","What is the immediate treatment of suspected herpes simplex encephalitis?","Intravenous aciclovir without waiting for confirmation","Delay greatly worsens outcome, and the drug is relatively safe."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Viral encephalitis","A 30-year-old man recovering from herpes encephalitis is hyperoral, hypersexual and placid. What is this syndrome?","Kluver-Bucy syndrome","Bilateral medial temporal damage to amygdala and uncus removes normal drive and fear regulation."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Viral encephalitis","From which experiment is Kluver-Bucy syndrome derived?","Bilateral temporal lobectomy in monkeys","Removing amygdala, uncus and hippocampus produced placidity, hyperorality and hypersexuality."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Viral encephalitis","Which childhood infection can cause subacute sclerosing panencephalitis years later?","Measles","Persistent defective virus causes slow panencephalitis with myoclonus and periodic EEG complexes."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Autoimmune encephalitis","A 23-year-old woman is admitted with new psychosis, then seizures, orofacial dyskinesia and autonomic instability. What diagnosis must be excluded?","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis","Antibodies remove NMDA receptors, causing psychosis first and then a movement and autonomic disorder."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Autoimmune encephalitis","Which tumour is associated with anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis in young women?","Ovarian teratoma","Neural tissue in the teratoma triggers the antibody response, so tumour removal helps recovery."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Autoimmune encephalitis","How is anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis treated?","Immunotherapy such as steroids, immunoglobulin or plasma exchange, plus tumour removal","Removing the antibody and its source allows receptors to be re-expressed."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Multiple sclerosis","Which disease is modelled by inducing experimental autoimmune encephalomyelitis in animals?","Multiple sclerosis","The model reproduces CNS inflammation and demyelination, so it is used to test treatments."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Multiple sclerosis","Which CSF finding supports multiple sclerosis?","Oligoclonal IgG bands not present in serum","Local antibody production within the CNS shows intrathecal immune activity."],
["CNS infection and neuroimmunology","Multiple sclerosis","Name two psychiatric presentations of multiple sclerosis.","Depression and pathological laughing or crying","Demyelination of frontal and subcortical circuits disturbs mood and its expression."],
/* ---------- Neuroendocrinology ---------- */
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","Trace the hormone sequence of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.","CRH, then ACTH, then cortisol","Each step amplifies the signal, and cortisol feeds back to switch the axis off."],
["Neuroendocrinology","HPA axis","What is the dexamethasone suppression test result in melancholic depression?","Non-suppression of cortisol","Negative feedback is blunted, so a synthetic steroid fails to switch off the axis."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Thyroid","A 42-year-old woman referred by her GP has dry skin, tiredness, bradycardia and poor concentration. What is the likely diagnosis?","Hypothyroidism","Low thyroid hormone slows metabolism everywhere, including cortical processing."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Thyroid","A 37-year-old man has palpitations, sweating and weight loss despite a good appetite with anxiety. What is the likely diagnosis?","Hyperthyroidism","Excess thyroid hormone raises adrenergic sensitivity, which mimics an anxiety disorder."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Thyroid","Why must thyroid function be monitored during lithium treatment?","Lithium can cause hypothyroidism and goitre","It blocks thyroid hormone release, so TSH rises over time."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal","A 36-year-old woman has hirsutism, weight gain, hypertension and depression. Which endocrine disorder is likely?","Cushing's syndrome","Excess cortisol causes the physical signs and is also directly depressogenic."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal","Which psychiatric presentation is commonest in Cushing's syndrome?","Depression","Mood disturbance often precedes the full physical picture and improves when cortisol falls."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal","Which endocrine disorder causes apathy, weight loss, low mood and skin pigmentation?","Addison's disease","Low cortisol with high ACTH produces fatigue, hypotension and pigmentation."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Adrenal","A 42-year-old woman is referred for panic attacks but has paroxysmal palpitations and high blood pressure. What must be excluded?","Phaeochromocytoma","Surges of catecholamines reproduce panic symptoms with measurable hypertension."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Calcium","A 35-year-old man has fatigue, muscle pain, thirst, low mood and a change in personality. Which endocrine disorder fits?","Hyperparathyroidism","Excess parathyroid hormone raises calcium, which causes thirst, polyuria and neuropsychiatric change."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Water balance","A 21-year-old man has polyuria and polydipsia with high plasma osmolality and dilute urine. What is the diagnosis?","Diabetes insipidus","Either ADH is lacking or the kidney cannot respond, so water is lost and plasma concentrates."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Water balance","Which psychotropic commonly causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?","Lithium","It interferes with ADH signalling in the collecting duct, so urine cannot be concentrated."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Water balance","Which psychotropic class most often causes hyponatraemia in older people?","SSRIs","They can trigger inappropriate ADH secretion, so water is retained and sodium falls."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Porphyria","A 30-year-old woman has recurrent abdominal pain, vomiting, confusion and dark red urine after drinking alcohol. What is the diagnosis?","Acute intermittent porphyria","A haem synthesis defect allows neurotoxic precursors to build up when the pathway is driven hard."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Porphyria","Which test confirms an acute attack of intermittent porphyria?","Raised urinary porphobilinogen during the attack","The blocked enzyme step causes this precursor to spill into urine."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Pituitary hormones","What normally inhibits prolactin release?","Dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular pathway","Prolactin is under tonic inhibition, so blocking D2 receptors raises it."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Neuropeptides","Which neuropeptide is linked to anxiety and can provoke panic attacks on infusion?","Cholecystokinin","CCK-B receptor activation in limbic areas triggers panic, and antagonists are anxiolytic in models."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Neuropeptides","Which neuropeptides regulate circadian function, arousal and feeding?","Orexins (hypocretins)","They are made in the lateral hypothalamus and stabilise the wake state."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Neuropeptides","Which function is beta-endorphin most associated with?","Analgesia and mood regulation","It is the most potent endogenous opioid and is released in pain and emotional arousal."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Neuropeptides","Which aspect of brain function is vasopressin thought to modulate besides water balance?","Cognition, including learning and memory","Central vasopressin receptors are found in hippocampus and cortex."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Neuropeptides","Which functions do the enkephalins mediate?","Pain perception, mood and neuroendocrine behaviour","They are endogenous opioid peptides acting at delta and mu receptors."],
["Neuroendocrinology","Appetite hormones","Name three endocrine findings in severe anorexia nervosa.","Low LH and FSH, raised cortisol and low T3","Starvation shuts down reproduction and converts thyroid hormone to the inactive form to save energy."],
/* ---------- Sleep ---------- */
["Sleep","Sleep architecture","Which EEG features define stage N2 sleep?","Sleep spindles and K complexes","Thalamocortical bursts produce spindles, which mark established light sleep."],
["Sleep","Sleep architecture","Which EEG activity defines stage N3 sleep?","High-amplitude delta activity below 4 Hz","Widespread cortical synchronisation produces large slow waves."],
["Sleep","Sleep architecture","Describe the EEG in REM sleep.","Low-voltage mixed frequency activity with sawtooth waves, resembling wakefulness","Cortex is active while the body is paralysed, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep."],
["Sleep","Sleep architecture","Why do we not act out dreams in normal REM sleep?","Because of active muscle atonia","Brainstem circuits inhibit spinal motor neurons during REM."],
["Sleep","Sleep and mood","Name three sleep EEG changes typical of major depression.","Shortened REM latency, increased REM density and reduced slow wave sleep","REM pressure appears to be released early, while deep restorative sleep is lost."],
["Sleep","Sleep and mood","What effect do most antidepressants have on REM sleep?","They suppress it","Raising monoamines inhibits REM-on cholinergic neurons, so REM is delayed and reduced."],
["Sleep","Sleep and mood","What is the effect of one night of total sleep deprivation in depression?","Transient mood improvement in some patients","The effect is short lived and usually lost after the next night's sleep."],
["Sleep","Circadian rhythm","Which hypothalamic nucleus sets the body's circadian rhythm?","The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus","It receives direct retinal input, so light sets its phase."],
["Sleep","Circadian rhythm","Which hormone signals darkness, and from where?","Melatonin, from the pineal gland","Light exposure suppresses its secretion, so it rises in the evening."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","What is the classic tetrad of narcolepsy?","Excessive daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations","All four are REM phenomena intruding into wakefulness."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","What is the underlying deficit in narcolepsy with cataplexy?","Loss of hypothalamic orexin (hypocretin) neurons","Without orexin the wake state is unstable, so REM features break through."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","Which test confirms narcolepsy?","The multiple sleep latency test showing short sleep latency and early REM","Objective measurement of how fast REM appears distinguishes it from other causes of sleepiness."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","A 62-year-old man punches and shouts during vivid dreams. What does REM sleep behaviour disorder predict?","An alpha-synuclein disorder such as Parkinson's disease or Lewy body dementia","Brainstem synuclein pathology removes REM atonia years before motor or cognitive signs."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","From which sleep stage does sleepwalking arise, and when in the night?","Stage N3, in the first third of the night","It is a partial arousal from deep slow wave sleep, which is concentrated early in the night."],
["Sleep","Sleep disorders","What is first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in the UK?","Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia and sleep hygiene","Hypnotics work briefly but risk tolerance and dependence, so behavioural change comes first."],
/* ---------- Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders ---------- */
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: dopamine","Which dopaminergic pathway is thought to be overactive in positive symptoms of schizophrenia?","The mesolimbic pathway","Excess striatal dopamine gives aberrant salience to irrelevant stimuli."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: dopamine","Which dopaminergic pathway is thought to be underactive in negative symptoms?","The mesocortical pathway to prefrontal cortex","Low prefrontal dopamine impairs drive, planning and working memory."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: dopamine","D2 occupancy in which region predicts antipsychotic response?","The corpus striatum","Striatal occupancy of roughly 65 to 80 per cent gives benefit, and above 80 per cent gives extrapyramidal effects."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: dopamine","Enlargement of which structure may follow long-term treatment with haloperidol?","The basal ganglia (corpus striatum)","Chronic D2 blockade causes compensatory hypertrophy, which can reverse on switching to an atypical drug."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: other systems","Which receptor is blocked by ketamine and phencyclidine to model schizophrenia?","The NMDA glutamate receptor","NMDA hypofunction on inhibitory interneurons disinhibits cortex and reproduces negative and cognitive symptoms."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: other systems","Which cortical interneuron population is reduced in schizophrenia?","Parvalbumin-containing GABA interneurons","These cells generate gamma oscillations, so their loss disturbs cortical timing."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: other systems","Which pathological feature is notably absent in schizophrenia brains?","Gliosis","Absence of a scar response argues for a neurodevelopmental rather than degenerative process."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: other systems","What does the reduced neuropil hypothesis propose?","Grey matter loss reflects fewer dendrites and synapses, not fewer neurons","Cell counts are largely preserved while spine density falls."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: markers","Which evoked potential abnormality indicates poor sensory gating in schizophrenia?","Reduced P50 suppression to paired clicks","Failure to dampen the second click shows that irrelevant input is not filtered."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Schizophrenia: markers","Which eye movement abnormality is a candidate endophenotype of schizophrenia?","Impaired smooth pursuit and antisaccade performance","Both need prefrontal control of reflexive gaze, which is a heritable weakness."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Depression","What does the tryptophan depletion experiment show?","It triggers relapse in people recently recovered on serotonergic antidepressants","Removing the precursor of serotonin lowers transmission, so the drug effect is reversed."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Depression","Which growth factor is reduced in depression and increased by antidepressants?","Brain-derived neurotrophic factor","BDNF supports synapse formation and hippocampal neurogenesis, which may underlie delayed response."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Depression","Which region is overactive in treatment-resistant depression and targeted by deep brain stimulation?","The subcallosal (subgenual) anterior cingulate cortex, Brodmann area 25","Overactivity here is linked to sustained negative affect, and stimulation reduces it."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","OCD and anxiety","Which circuit is overactive in obsessive-compulsive disorder?","The cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loop, with orbitofrontal and caudate hyperactivity","A failure of the loop to terminate signals keeps the urge to act switched on."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","OCD and anxiety","Which deep brain stimulation target is used in treatment-resistant OCD?","The anterior limb of the internal capsule","Fibres linking orbitofrontal cortex to striatum run here, so stimulation interrupts the loop."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","OCD and anxiety","Which nucleus is central to the noradrenergic alarm response in panic?","The locus coeruleus","It drives cortical arousal and autonomic activation when stimulated."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","OCD and anxiety","Which inhalation challenge provokes panic in panic disorder?","Carbon dioxide inhalation","Hypersensitive brainstem chemoreceptors read rising CO2 as suffocation."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","OCD and anxiety","Which structures are abnormal in post-traumatic stress disorder?","Hyperactive amygdala with reduced hippocampal volume and poor medial prefrontal control","Fear is over-signalled and under-regulated, so extinction fails."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Addiction","Which pathway mediates the reinforcing effect of drugs of abuse?","The mesolimbic dopamine projection to the nucleus accumbens","Nearly all addictive drugs raise accumbens dopamine, which marks the act as worth repeating."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Addiction","Which two receptor changes explain alcohol withdrawal seizures?","Down-regulated GABA-A and up-regulated NMDA receptors","Removing alcohol leaves a brain with weak inhibition and strong excitation."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Neurodevelopmental disorders","Which circuits are implicated in ADHD?","Prefrontal-striatal circuits using dopamine and noradrenaline","Weak top-down control of the striatum impairs inhibition and sustained attention."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Delirium and catatonia","Which neurotransmitter imbalance is proposed in delirium?","Cholinergic deficiency with relative dopamine excess","This explains why anticholinergic drugs precipitate delirium and antipsychotics can help."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Delirium and catatonia","A 28-year-old woman on an acute ward is mute, immobile and shows waxy flexibility. Which drug challenge is both diagnostic and therapeutic?","Lorazepam","Catatonia is associated with reduced GABA-A function in motor cortex, so a GABA agonist relieves it."],
["Applied neuroscience of psychiatric disorders","Delirium and catatonia","What happens to brain glucose metabolism immediately after an ECT-induced seizure?","It falls, with a period of post-ictal suppression","The seizure exhausts cortical activity, and this anticonvulsant effect may be therapeutic."],
/* ---------- Research methods and brain stimulation ---------- */
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Animal models","Which animal test measures behavioural despair and is used to screen antidepressants?","The forced swim test","Immobility time is taken as helplessness and is reduced by antidepressant drugs."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Animal models","Which maze task is used to test hippocampal spatial memory?","The Morris water maze","The animal must use distant cues to find a hidden platform, which requires the hippocampus."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Electrophysiology","Which recording technique measures current through a single ion channel?","Patch clamp recording","A micropipette forms a tight seal on a patch of membrane, isolating a few channels."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Electrophysiology","Which technique measures subthreshold potentials such as EPSPs in a single neuron?","Intracellular recording","The electrode sits inside the cell, so small graded potentials can be seen."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Electrophysiology","Which technique best measures coordinated neuronal spiking in vivo?","Extracellular recording","An electrode just outside the cells detects spikes and local field potentials from a population."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","What are human induced pluripotent stem cells?","Adult somatic cells reprogrammed to an embryonic stem cell-like state","Reprogrammed skin fibroblasts can be grown into neurons, so patient-specific cells can be studied."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","What is the main advantage of induced pluripotent stem cells over embryonic stem cells?","They avoid the ethical problem of destroying embryos","No blastocyst is needed, since the cells come from an adult donor."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","What does the CLARITY method achieve?","It makes intact brain tissue optically transparent using a hydrogel","Light-scattering lipids are replaced by hydrogel, so whole brains can be imaged without slicing."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","What is optogenetics?","Using light to activate or inhibit neurons expressing light-sensitive channels","Genetically targeted channels make it possible to control defined cell types with millisecond precision."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","Which colour of light opens channelrhodopsin-2 and depolarises neurons?","Blue light","Channelrhodopsin-2 is a blue light-gated cation channel, so cations enter and the cell fires."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Cell and tissue methods","Which receptor does clozapine-N-oxide act on in DREADD experiments?","The designer M3 muscarinic receptor","The drug is otherwise inert, so only cells engineered to carry the designer receptor respond."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","Which cortical target and frequency are standard for rTMS in depression?","Left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex at 10 Hz","High-frequency stimulation of the hypoactive left prefrontal cortex is the best established protocol."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","What is the commonest adverse effect of rTMS?","Scalp pain or headache","Stimulation of scalp muscles and nerves causes discomfort that usually settles with simple analgesia."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","Which rTMS frequencies are excitatory and which inhibitory?","High frequency 5 to 20 Hz is excitatory; low frequency 1 to 5 Hz is inhibitory","Faster trains facilitate synapses while slow trains depress them, like long-term potentiation and depression."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","Why is the motor threshold measured before giving TMS?","To set the dose for each individual","It is the lowest intensity producing a motor evoked potential in half of trials, so it calibrates for skull and cortex differences."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","How does rTMS compare with ECT for treatment-resistant depression?","It is not superior to ECT in efficacy","ECT gives higher response and remission rates, especially in psychotic depression, but rTMS has fewer memory effects."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","Which prefrontal stimulation protocol reduces the GABA to glutamate ratio in the DLPFC?","Intermittent theta-burst stimulation","Short high-frequency bursts in a theta rhythm produce potentiation-like changes in local inhibition."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","What does tDCS stand for?","Transcranial direct current stimulation","A weak constant current shifts resting membrane potential without inducing action potentials."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","A 53-year-old man with treatment-resistant OCD has a pacemaker under his collarbone that his psychiatrist switches on. Which treatment is this?","Deep brain stimulation","Intracranial electrodes are connected by subcutaneous wires to a programmable pulse generator."],
["Research methods and brain stimulation","Brain stimulation","What is known about rTMS for depression in people over 65?","Evidence is insufficient because older people were excluded from most trials","Lack of data is not the same as lack of effect, so it is a gap rather than a negative finding."],
/* ---------- Head injury and cognitive localisation ---------- */
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","A 25-year-old man has a severe head injury in a motorcycle crash and afterwards is disinhibited and tactless. Which injury is most likely on MRI?","Bilateral orbitofrontal contre-coup contusions","The orbital surface strikes the rough bony floor of the skull as the brain moves, so this area is injured even when the blow is occipital."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","Which injury explains prolonged coma after trauma with a normal initial CT?","Diffuse axonal injury","Shearing forces tear axons throughout the white matter, which may not show on early CT."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","A 25-year-old man is slow to shift between tasks after a severe head injury. Which test is most likely to be abnormal?","The Wisconsin card sorting test","It measures set shifting, which depends on dorsolateral prefrontal cortex."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","Which neuropathological changes are found in dementia pugilistica?","Neurofibrillary tangles with neuronal loss","Repeated concussion also thins the corpus callosum, perforates the septum pellucidum and widens the ventricles."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","Which test estimates premorbid IQ after a head injury?","The National Adult Reading Test","Reading irregular words relies on knowledge acquired before the injury, which is relatively spared."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","What are the three clinical features of the vegetative state?","Cycles of eye opening and closing, no awareness of self or environment, and preserved brainstem autonomic function","Arousal mechanisms survive while cortical awareness does not."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Traumatic brain injury","A 63-year-old man is quadriplegic and mute after a road accident but answers questions by blinking. Where is the lesion?","The ventral pons","Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibres are cut while the midbrain gaze centres and consciousness survive."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal syndromes","Which frontal syndrome follows orbitofrontal damage?","A pseudopsychopathic syndrome with disinhibition and poor impulse control","Loss of orbitofrontal inhibition releases socially inappropriate behaviour."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal syndromes","Which syndrome follows dorsolateral prefrontal damage?","A dysexecutive syndrome with poor planning, judgement and insight","This region holds and manipulates information for goal-directed behaviour."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal syndromes","Which syndrome follows ventromedial or medial frontal damage?","An apathetic, pseudodepressive syndrome","Sparse spontaneous behaviour and sparse speech occur, often with incontinence."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal syndromes","Which lesion causes forced utilisation behaviour?","An orbitofrontal lesion (with caudate involvement)","Objects in view trigger their use automatically because inhibitory control is lost."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Which bedside test specifically measures response inhibition?","The go-no-go test","The patient must withhold the natural response, which requires inferior frontal control."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Which test measures both response initiation and suppression using sentence completion?","The Hayling sentence completion test","Timed sensible completion tests initiation, and nonsense completion tests suppression."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Asking how tall an average English woman is tests which function?","Cognitive estimates, a frontal executive function","Frontal patients give wildly improbable answers because they cannot check plausibility."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Naming as many words as possible beginning with F in one minute tests what?","Lexical (phonological) fluency","Searching by sound rather than meaning relies on left frontal strategy generation."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Which part of the trail making test is more sensitive to frontal dysfunction?","Part B, which alternates numbers and letters","Alternating between two sequences adds a set-shifting load."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Frontal lobe testing","Loss of verbal fluency points to a lesion in which region?","The left frontal lobe","Broca's area and nearby cortex organise word production."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","A 78-year-old man leaves the food on one side of his plate after a stroke. Where is the lesion?","The right parietal lobe","The non-dominant inferior parietal cortex maps space on both sides, so its loss produces left neglect."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","Which lesion causes Gerstmann syndrome?","A lesion of the dominant parietal lobe, at the angular gyrus","Agraphia, dyscalculia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation cluster there."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","A 41-year-old woman cannot recognise faces after a head injury. Where is the damage?","The bilateral or right occipitotemporal junction, at the fusiform gyrus","The fusiform face area specialises in configural face processing."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","Which lesion causes alexia without agraphia?","Left posterior cerebral artery infarction damaging the splenium and left visual cortex","Visual input in the right hemisphere cannot reach left-sided language areas, so reading fails while writing is spared."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","What are the three components of Balint's syndrome?","Simultanagnosia, optic ataxia and oculomotor apraxia","Bilateral parieto-occipital damage breaks the dorsal where stream that guides looking and reaching."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Localisation of lesions","A 57-year-old man is blind after a stroke but denies it and tries to walk unaided. What is this called?","Anton syndrome","Cortical blindness with denial occurs when the damaged visual cortex cannot inform monitoring systems."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Aphasia and agnosia","Which aphasia has non-fluent speech with impaired repetition but preserved comprehension?","Broca's aphasia","Damage to the inferior frontal output area leaves understanding intact but speech laboured and telegraphic."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Aphasia and agnosia","A 74-year-old man calls apples oranges after a stroke. What is this called?","Semantic paraphasia","The chosen word is conceptually related, which shows a fault in lexical selection rather than in sound."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Aphasia and agnosia","A 65-year-old man cannot identify keys by touch with his eyes closed but knows them by sight. What is this?","Astereognosis","Parietal cortex integrates tactile shape and texture into recognition."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Aphasia and agnosia","Inability to recognise a number traced on the skin is called what?","Agraphaesthesia (loss of graphaesthesia)","Recognising a traced figure is a parietal association function."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Aphasia and agnosia","A 73-year-old man with left hemiplegia denies any problem walking. What is this called?","Anosognosia","Non-dominant parietal damage removes the ability to monitor one's own deficit."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Apraxias","A 70-year-old electrician recovers full strength after a left hemisphere stroke but cannot use his tools. What is this?","Ideomotor apraxia","He knows what to do but not how to do it, and performance improves with imitation and the real tool."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Apraxias","A 68-year-old man cannot carry out a multi-step task although single imitated actions are fine. What is this?","Ideational apraxia","The plan itself is lost, so the correct sequence of object use cannot be assembled."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Apraxias","Inability to copy intersecting pentagons on the MMSE is which deficit?","Constructional apraxia (visuospatial deficit)","Assembling parts into a whole depends mainly on the non-dominant parietal lobe."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Apraxias","A 63-year-old man with young-onset dementia cannot put on a jumper that is inside out. What is this?","Dressing apraxia","Mapping clothing onto the body needs non-dominant parietal spatial processing."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Hemisphere specialisation","Which functions are bilateral rather than strictly localised?","Face recognition, memory and personality","Broad constructs depend on distributed networks in both hemispheres."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Memory and amnesia","What does Ribot's law state?","Recent memories are lost before remote memories in organic amnesia","Older memories have been consolidated and rehearsed repeatedly, so they are more robust."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Memory and amnesia","A 40-year-old woman is found wandering after her husband's funeral; she gives her own details and has no injury. Which deficit is expected?","Loss of well-rehearsed personal memories","Dissociative amnesia selectively affects autobiographical material, which is the reverse of an organic pattern."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Memory and amnesia","An 83-year-old woman cannot learn new information. Which structure is likely damaged?","The medial temporal lobe, containing the hippocampus","The hippocampus is needed to lay down new episodic memories."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Which domain of orientation is affected first in delirium?","Orientation to time","Time needs continuous updating, so it fails before place and person."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Spelling WORLD backwards tests which function?","Attention","Holding and manipulating the letters requires sustained attention and working memory."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Which test distinguishes organic from psychiatric stupor?","Caloric (vestibular) testing","Organic stupor gives tonic deviation only, while nystagmus needs a conscious cortex to make the fast corrective phase."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","An 18-year-old girl has sudden blindness with normal fundi and normal visual evoked potentials. Which field finding supports a conversion disorder?","Tunnel vision","A field that does not widen with distance is not anatomically possible."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Which WAIS subtests are 'hold' tests resistant to age-related decline?","Vocabulary, information, object assembly and picture completion","They draw on stored knowledge, whereas block design and digit span decline earlier."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Which IQ test is least affected by language and culture?","Raven's progressive matrices","It uses non-verbal pattern completion, so limited English does not penalise the person."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","Which test measures everyday memory to guide rehabilitation after brain injury?","The Rivermead behavioural memory test","It uses tasks like remembering a route or a name, which mirror daily demands."],
["Head injury and cognitive localisation","Cognitive testing","A 60-year-old woman scores 26 out of 30 on the MMSE but keeps saying she cannot be bothered to answer. Which instrument should be added?","The Beck depression inventory","Depression can mimic dementia, and 'don't know' responses are typical of depressive pseudodementia."],
]);
